────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/config set and the "always allow" writer now edit config through toml_edit, and on my test corpus nothing they weren't asked to change moved by a byte. It's committed as 33dc898d, titled "Part of #1184". Build, all tests, clippy with warnings as errors and the format check all pass.

What was broken before (measured on today's code before any change; table posted on #1184):
• Multi-line allow list: "always allow" rewrote only the allow = [ line and left the old entries and ] dangling, which made the file invalid TOML. The slice comment had predicted this; it's real.
• Files with a section: a new top-level key was added at the end of the file, which put it inside the last [section]. The run only reads top-level keys, so a value /config set reported as set was never used. Nobody had filed this one.
• Same key under a header: /config set model replaced the model under [permissions] instead of setting the top-level one.
• Comments: a comment beside a replaced value (model = "a" # beside) was dropped.
• Escaping: allow patterns were wrapped in quotes without escaping, so a " in a pattern broke the file.
• Broken files: a file that wasn't valid TOML got written over anyway.

What changed:
• New `src/config_toml_write.rs`: holds both writers, with the before/after corpus as tests. The old line-editing code is deleted from config.rs (4700 → 4548 lines, size cap lowered to match).
• New dependency: toml_edit 0.25.19. It reuses the TOML crates the existing toml dependency already brings in, so nothing is pulled in twice.
• Comments, order and spelling survive: quoted keys ("model"), dotted permissions.allow and inline tables are kept as written. A new allow entry goes on its own line in a multi-line list. A new top-level key goes before the first section.

Behaviour changes you'll notice:
• Invalid files are refused: if the file isn't valid TOML, both writers leave it byte-identical and the error names the file and the parser's line and column. A test covers each writer.
• Different quoting: toml_edit picks how strings are quoted, so a value with a quote or backslash is now written as 'say "hi"' rather than with backslash escapes. Reading it back gives the exact value. I rewrote three existing tests to expect this (the two format_toml_value tests and the quoted-key spelling test); none were deleted.
• Table-name clashes refused: a key that clashes with a table, a permissions that isn't a table, or an allow that isn't a list now gets an error instead of being overwritten.

Checks:
• Recall control: I broke the allow writer on purpose so it rewrote the list on one line. The multi-line test case failed by name, and the suite went green again after I restored it.
• Built binary: in the REPL, /config set model claude-x on a commented .yoyo.toml changed only the value and kept the comment. yoyo config set from the shell is still interactive-only, as before.
• Not driven: I didn't test the broken-file refusal through the REPL, because a broken config already stops yoyo at startup. The unit tests cover it.

#1184 progress: the line-reading count in the three config files went from 14 to 12. Before, after and close-out comments are on #1184. Still open there, which is why the commit says "Part of": the parse_toml_array callers in cli.rs and cd_config_note.rs, the line walkers in config_value and config_unknown_keys, and deleting the tomllib comparison test. I also added a note for the new file to ARCHITECTURE.md and marked its old claim about the deleted writer as superseded.

